## Common Pitfalls with ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs – and How to Dodge Them

Minimal APIs (the `builder.MapXXX()` style introduced in .NET 6) are a fantastic way to spin up tiny, fast services, but they’re also a quick path to “everything in one file” hell. Below is a consolidated cheat‑sheet of the most frequent mistakes, why they’re problematic, and concrete ways to avoid them.

| # | Mistake | Why It Matters | How to Avoid / Fix |
|---|---------|----------------|--------------------|
| 1 | **Not organizing endpoints into groups** | A single file can grow into a “God‑file” that’s hard to read, test, and maintain. | Use `MapGroup()` to split routes by area, domain, or HTTP verb. Apply common middleware (`RequireAuthorization()`, `RequireRateLimiting()`) once per group. |
| 2 | **Mixing sync & async for I/O operations** | Synchronous I/O blocks the thread pool, hurting throughput. | Mark handlers as `async Task<IResult>` (or `ValueTask<IResult>`) and use `await` for database, HTTP, or file I/O. |
| 3 | **Missing or mis‑configuring DI** | Null references, service resolution errors, or leaking scopes. | Register services in `builder.Services` before `builder.Build()`. Prefer constructor‑style DI for larger projects (`app.MapGet("/", ([FromServices] IMyService svc) => ...)` or extension methods). |
| 4 | **Ignoring input validation** | Validation bugs become security holes (SQL injection, over‑posting, etc.). | Use `IValidator<T>` with FluentValidation, or data‑annotation attributes. Call `context.Validate()` or `context.Request.Body.From<>()` with validation. |
| 5 | **Hard‑coding status codes** | You lose flexibility, readability, and testability. | Return typed results (`Results.Ok(...)`, `Results.NotFound()`) or `IResult` from a method that knows the contract. |
| 6 | **No global error handling** | Unhandled exceptions leak stack traces to clients. | Use `app.UseExceptionHandler()` or `app.UseMiddleware<ErrorHandlingMiddleware>()`. Or `app.MapFallback()` to return a generic error response. |
| 7 | **Neglecting authentication/authorization** | Open endpoints are vulnerable. | Apply `[Authorize]` or `RequireAuthorization()` per endpoint or group. Use policy‑based auth (`RequireAuthorization("AdminPolicy")`). |
| 8 | **Skipping CORS** | Front‑end apps cannot consume the API in browsers. | Register `builder.Services.AddCors()` and `app.UseCors()` early. Use named policies. |
| 9 | **Improper use of `MapGet` with incorrect signature** | Binding failures, confusing route templates, or runtime exceptions. | Keep the lambda signature in sync with the route pattern: `app.MapGet("/items/{id:int}", (int id, IItemRepo repo) => ...)`. |
|10 | **Not leveraging endpoint metadata** | Swagger/OpenAPI docs become incomplete or misleading. | Use `.WithName("GetItem")`, `.WithTags("Items")`, `.Produces<T>()`, `.Consumes<T>()`. |
|11 | **Missing health checks** | Deployment pipelines or orchestrators cannot verify service health. | Add `builder.Services.AddHealthChecks()`, register probes, and expose `/health`. |
|12 | **Wrong middleware order** | e.g., `UseRouting`/`UseEndpoints` after `UseAuthorization`. | Minimal APIs handle this automatically, but if you intermix with classic middleware, keep the canonical order: authentication → authorization → routing → endpoints. |
|13 | **Unnecessary static state** | Thread‑safety issues, memory leaks, and test flakiness. | Avoid `static` fields for request‑related data. Use scoped services or `AsyncLocal<T>`. |
|14 | **Ignoring content negotiation** | Clients get wrong `Content-Type` or malformed payloads. | Return `Results.Json()`, `Results.Text()`, `Results.Bytes()` explicitly or rely on built‑in formatters. |
|15 | **Missing versioning** | Clients may break when the API evolves. | Use route prefixes (`/v1/...`, `/v2/...`) or custom conventions. Implement `IEndpointConventionBuilder.MapGroup` with a `MapWithMetadata` that adds a `Metadata` tag. |
|16 | **Skipping logging** | Hard to diagnose production issues. | Inject `ILogger<Program>` or create a scoped logger per endpoint (`ILoggerFactory`). Use structured logging (`logger.LogInformation("Fetching item {Id}", id);`). |
|17 | **Inadequate testing** | Minimal APIs can be difficult to unit‑test in isolation. | Use `WebApplicationFactory<TEntryPoint>` for integration tests. For unit tests, extract the business logic into services and mock those. |
|18 | **Using minimal APIs for complex scenarios** | Lack of attributes, model binding, filters, etc., can make code unreadable. | Keep minimal APIs for simple CRUD or micro‑services. Use controllers when you need filters, complex validation, or action result variations. |
|19 | **Deploying without HTTPS** | Client traffic is unencrypted. | Enforce HTTPS with `builder.WebHost.UseKestrel(options => options.ConfigureHttpsDefaults(...))` or `app.UseHttpsRedirection()`. |
|20 | **Ignoring resource limits** | Unbounded data loads can crash the app. | Use request size limits (`app.Use((ctx, next) => { ctx.Request.EnableBuffering(); return next(); })`), or limit query parameters with `[FromQuery]` validations. |

